# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
A(('h1', S1))
A(('p', 'By the end of this study, you should be able to:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Read the mustard seed in each of its three accounts, Matthew 13:31–32, Mark 4:30–32 and Luke 13:18–19, in the order and setting each gospel gives it, and the leaven in Matthew 13:33 and Luke 13:20–21.',
    'Mark every difference between the accounts as a difference, and refuse to carry a detail from one account into another.',
    'Mark every part of the stories that Jesus leaves unnamed, and see that none of the three accounts records an explanation from Him.',
    'State what the two comparisons say about the Kingdom without saying what the tree, the branches, the birds, the leaven, the woman, the meal or the three measures stand for.',
    'Place the parables in Jesus’ earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and hold the boundary where the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins.',
    'State a principled lesson for today and say plainly why the sowing, the hiding and the growing in these stories are not steps of salvation.',
]))
A(('call', 'Study Goal',
   'To read the mustard seed and the leaven exactly as Matthew, Mark and Luke give them, to mark what Jesus does not identify, and to state what the comparisons say about the Kingdom without adding a referent, a symbol or a doctrine the text does not supply.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 Matthew’s setting. In Matthew the mustard seed and the leaven belong to the day of parables. “The same day went Jesus out of the house, and sat by the sea side” (13:1). He spoke to the multitude from a ship, and “the whole multitude stood on the shore” (13:2). The mustard seed is introduced with “Another parable put he forth unto them” (13:31), and the leaven with “Another parable spake he unto them” (13:33). Matthew then closes the multitude’s part of the day: “All these things spake Jesus unto the multitude in parables; and without a parable spake he not unto them” (13:34). Only after both parables does he write, “Then Jesus sent the multitude away, and went into the house” (13:36). In Matthew, then, the mustard seed and the leaven are spoken to the multitude, before the move into the house.'))
A(('p', '2.2 Mark’s setting. Mark gives the mustard seed alone, with no leaven. It stands in Mark’s chapter of parables: “And he began again to teach by the sea side: and there was gathered unto him a great multitude” (4:1). Mark says, “he taught them many things by parables” (4:2). The mustard seed opens with a question that Mark puts in Jesus’ mouth: “Whereunto shall we liken the kingdom of God? or with what comparison shall we compare it?” (4:30). Mark then writes of “many such parables” spoken “as they were able to hear it” (4:33), and adds, “when they were alone, he expounded all things to his disciples” (4:34).'))
A(('p', '2.3 Luke’s setting. Luke does not place these parables on a shore or among a chapter of seed parables. “He was teaching in one of the synagogues on the sabbath” (13:10). A woman “which had a spirit of infirmity eighteen years” was healed (13:11–13), the ruler of the synagogue objected (13:14), the Lord answered (13:15–16), and “all his adversaries were ashamed: and all the people rejoiced for all the glorious things that were done by him” (13:17). Luke continues, “Then said he, Unto what is the kingdom of God like? and whereunto shall I resemble it?” (13:18). The leaven follows with “And again he said, Whereunto shall I liken the kingdom of God?” (13:20). Luke then writes that Jesus “went through the cities and villages, teaching, and journeying toward Jerusalem” (13:22).'))
A(('call', 'Three Settings, Not One',
   'Matthew places the parables among the multitude by the sea. Mark places the mustard seed in a chapter of parables told by the sea. Luke places the mustard seed and the leaven after a healing in a synagogue. The accounts are read each in its own setting, and a detail of one setting is not carried into another.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Each gospel has already said what Jesus preached. Matthew: “Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). Mark: “The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand: repent ye, and believe the gospel” (1:15). Matthew records the Twelve’s charge, “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:5–6), and the message they carried, “The kingdom of heaven is at hand” (10:7). The parables speak of the same Kingdom to hearers who have been told it is at hand.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus speaks in parables here. Matthew records the disciples’ question and the answer: “Why speakest thou unto them in parables?” “Because it is given unto you to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it is not given” (13:10–11). Matthew also connects the day’s parables with the prophet’s words, “I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter things which have been kept secret from the foundation of the world” (13:35). These parables are not free-floating sayings about growth or influence in general. They are part of what Jesus uttered on that day to those who heard that the Kingdom was at hand.'))
A(('ctbl', 'The verses that set the scenes are laid out below.', [
    ['Verse', 'KJV text', 'What to observe'],
    ['Matt 13:1–2', '“The same day went Jesus out of the house”', 'The day of parables begins by the sea.'],
    ['Matt 13:34', '“All these things spake Jesus unto the multitude in parables”', 'The multitude hears these parables.'],
    ['Matt 13:36', '“Then Jesus sent the multitude away, and went into the house”', 'The move indoors comes after both parables.'],
    ['Mark 4:1–2', '“a great multitude”; “he taught them many things by parables”', 'The setting of Mark’s mustard seed.'],
    ['Mark 4:34', '“when they were alone, he expounded all things to his disciples”', 'A private exposition is mentioned; its content is not given.'],
    ['Luke 13:10', '“He was teaching in one of the synagogues on the sabbath”', 'The setting of Luke’s two parables.'],
    ['Luke 13:18', '“Then said he, Unto what is the kingdom of God like?”', 'Luke’s opening question.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parables are read here one account at a time, in the order each gospel gives. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another account, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 Matthew’s mustard seed (13:31–32). “Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took, and sowed in his field: Which indeed is the least of all seeds: but when it is grown, it is the greatest among herbs, and becometh a tree, so that the birds of the air come and lodge in the branches thereof.” The sequence is plain. A man takes a grain of mustard seed. He sows it in his field. The seed is the least of all seeds. When it is grown it is the greatest among herbs and becomes a tree. The birds of the air come and lodge in its branches.'))
A(('p', '3.2 Matthew’s leaven (13:33). “Another parable spake he unto them; The kingdom of heaven is like unto leaven, which a woman took, and hid in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened.” A woman takes leaven. She hides it in three measures of meal. The action runs “till the whole was leavened.” Matthew writes nothing after that.'))
A(('p', '3.3 Mark’s mustard seed (4:30–32). “And he said, Whereunto shall we liken the kingdom of God? or with what comparison shall we compare it? It is like a grain of mustard seed, which, when it is sown in the earth, is less than all the seeds that be in the earth: But when it is sown, it groweth up, and becometh greater than all herbs, and shooteth out great branches; so that the fowls of the air may lodge under the shadow of it.” Mark opens with two questions. The seed is sown in the earth and is less than all the seeds in the earth. When sown, it grows up, becomes greater than all herbs and shoots out great branches. The fowls of the air may lodge under its shadow. Mark names no man who takes the seed, no field and no tree.'))
A(('p', '3.4 Luke’s mustard seed (13:18–19). “Then said he, Unto what is the kingdom of God like? and whereunto shall I resemble it? It is like a grain of mustard seed, which a man took, and cast into his garden; and it grew, and waxed a great tree; and the fowls of the air lodged in the branches of it.” A man takes a grain of mustard seed and casts it into his garden. It grows and waxes a great tree. The fowls of the air lodged in its branches. Luke gives no comparison of the seed’s size to other seeds.'))
A(('p', '3.5 Luke’s leaven (13:20–21). “And again he said, Whereunto shall I liken the kingdom of God? It is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened.” The woman takes the leaven and hides it in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The differences laid side by side. The accounts share a grain of mustard seed that grows large and a comparison for the Kingdom. They differ in what the text shows.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Point of comparison', 'What the accounts say', 'What to observe'],
    ['Kingdom', 'Matthew “of heaven”; Mark and Luke “of God”', 'Each gospel uses its own words, and the difference is not explained.'],
    ['Who acts', 'Matthew and Luke: “a man took”; Mark: no man is named', 'In Mark the seed is “sown in the earth.”'],
    ['Where', 'Matthew: “his field”; Mark: “the earth”; Luke: “his garden”', 'Three different places are named.'],
    ['Size of seed', 'Matthew: “the least of all seeds”; Mark: “less than all the seeds that be in the earth”; Luke: not stated', 'Luke gives no comparison of size.'],
    ['What it becomes', 'Matthew: “the greatest among herbs, and becometh a tree”; Mark: “greater than all herbs” with “great branches”; Luke: “a great tree”', 'Matthew and Luke say tree; Mark says herb and branches.'],
    ['The birds', 'Matthew: “the birds of the air come and lodge in the branches”; Mark: “the fowls of the air may lodge under the shadow”; Luke: “the fowls of the air lodged in the branches”', 'Matthew and Luke say branches, Mark says shadow, and each has its own verb.'],
    ['The leaven', 'Matthew 13:33 and Luke 13:20–21 only', 'Mark has no leaven.'],
], [0.17, 0.50, 0.33]))
A(('p', '3.7 What the stories leave unsaid. None of the accounts says who the man is, what the field, the earth or the garden is, what the tree or the branches are, or what the birds or the fowls are. None says who the woman is, what the leaven is, or what the meal or the three measures are. None says how long the growth or the leavening takes, and none says that the leavened meal is baked, eaten or given to anyone. The stories give the taking, the sowing or hiding, the growth or the leavening, and they stop.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 Jesus gives no explanation. In none of the three accounts does the text record an explanation of the mustard seed or of the leaven. Matthew records the disciples’ request after the move into the house, “Declare unto us the parable of the tares of the field” (13:36), and it names a different parable. Mark says that “when they were alone, he expounded all things to his disciples” (4:34), but he gives no content of any exposition and does not say that the mustard seed was among the things expounded. Luke records no request and no answer. This section therefore records no identification, because there is none to record.'))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus does not identify. Jesus does not identify the man who takes the seed. Jesus does not identify the field, the earth or the garden. Jesus does not identify the mustard seed itself as anything beyond what He compares to the Kingdom. Jesus does not identify the tree, the herb or the branches. Jesus does not identify the birds or the fowls of the air, or what their lodging is. Jesus does not identify the woman. Jesus does not identify the leaven. Jesus does not identify the meal or the three measures. Jesus does not identify the leavening of the whole as anything beyond what the story says.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the story', 'What Jesus says it is', 'Verse'],
    ['The man who takes the seed', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Matt 13:31; Luke 13:19'],
    ['The field, the earth, the garden', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 13:31; Mark 4:31; Luke 13:19'],
    ['The tree, the herb, the branches', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Matt 13:32; Mark 4:32; Luke 13:19'],
    ['The birds, the fowls of the air', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Matt 13:32; Mark 4:32; Luke 13:19'],
    ['The woman', 'Jesus does not identify her.', 'Matt 13:33; Luke 13:21'],
    ['The leaven', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 13:33; Luke 13:21'],
    ['The meal, the three measures', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Matt 13:33; Luke 13:21'],
], [0.34, 0.34, 0.32]))
A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives no identification, none is given here. A reader who says that the tree is a certain body, that the birds are certain peoples, that the leaven is a certain influence or that the woman or the meal stands for something is supplying what the Lord did not. A detail that is explained elsewhere for another parable is not carried into these.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What the text does say. The text says that the Kingdom is like a grain of mustard seed that a man took and sowed, that it is the least or less than all seeds, and that it grows into the greatest among herbs, a tree, with branches in which birds or fowls lodge. It says that the Kingdom is like leaven that a woman took and hid in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened. These statements are made by the stories themselves and are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'No explanation is given for these parables, so this section states only the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It assigns no identity to any part of either story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus says the Kingdom is like a grain of mustard seed and like leaven. The seed is small beside the other seeds and large when grown. The leaven is hidden in meal and works until the whole is leavened. The meaning of the comparison is in the contrast between the small beginning and the large result, and in the hidden working that reaches the whole.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the Kingdom.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The beginning is small.** The seed is “the least of all seeds” (Matthew 13:32) and “less than all the seeds that be in the earth” (Mark 4:31). Luke gives no comparison of size, and the comparison of size is not carried into his account.',
    '**The result is large.** The seed “becometh a tree” (Matthew 13:32), becomes “greater than all herbs” (Mark 4:32) or “waxed a great tree” (Luke 13:19). The measure of the result is out of proportion to the beginning.',
    '**The change takes place by growth.** The seed grows, and the text says nothing of the man’s work after he sows or casts it. The comparison speaks of what the seed becomes.',
    '**The working can be hidden.** The woman “hid” the leaven (Matthew 13:33; Luke 13:21). What she does is out of sight, and the text gives the outcome, not the process.',
    '**The working reaches the whole.** The leaven works “till the whole was leavened.” The text states the extent and says nothing of the time it takes.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers had been told that the Kingdom was at hand (Matthew 4:17; Mark 1:15). The comparisons tell them that the Kingdom they were told of begins small and does not stay small, and that what is hidden can be complete in its effect. Luke’s hearers have just seen a woman set free in a synagogue and the glorious things done (13:17), and Luke gives the parable straight after. The text does not draw a line from the healing to the parable, and this study draws none.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is the force of contrast. A grain that is small beside every other seed becomes the largest of the herbs, or a tree, and a lump of leaven hidden in three measures of meal reaches the whole. The parables ask the hearer to look at the end of the story and not only at the beginning.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. The Kingdom begins small. The result is large. The working may be hidden. The working reaches the whole. These statements arise from the comparison, and they do not say that the seed, the tree, the birds, the leaven, the woman or the meal represents anything.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say what the tree, the branches or the birds are, and it does not say what the leaven, the woman, the meal or the three measures are. It does not say whether the growth is good or bad, quick or slow. It does not turn sowing, hiding or growing into steps by which a person is saved. It does not carry a meaning from another parable into these two.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The mustard seed and the leaven are told close to other seed and Kingdom parables. They are kept apart from their neighbors here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 13:3–9, 18–23; Mark 4:3–20', '“a sower went forth to sow”', 'The Sower and the Soils. Seed is sown and Jesus explains it. That explanation belongs to that parable only and is not carried to the mustard seed.'],
    ['Mark 4:26–29', '“a man should cast seed into the ground”', 'The Growing Seed. Mark’s own seed parable, just before the mustard seed. The man sleeps and rises and does not know how the seed grows, and the harvest follows. It has no tree and no birds.'],
    ['Matt 13:24–30, 36–43', '“sowed good seed in his field”', 'The Wheat and the Weeds. A field with an enemy and a harvest, and Jesus explains it. Its field and its explanation are not carried to the field of Matthew 13:31.'],
    ['Matt 13:44–46', '“treasure hid in a field”; “one pearl of great price”', 'The Hidden Treasure and the Pearl. These come after the move into the house. A man finds, sells and buys. No growth, and no leaven.'],
    ['Luke 12:1', '“the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy”', 'Another saying about leaven, in another setting. Jesus names it in that saying. That naming is not carried to the leaven of Luke 13:21.'],
], [0.22, 0.28, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. Matthew’s two parables are told to the multitude by the sea, before the move into the house. Mark’s mustard seed stands among his seed parables. Luke’s two parables follow a synagogue healing. A shared word such as “leaven” or “seed” does not make two settings one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The mustard seed has a man who takes and sows or casts. The leaven has a woman who takes and hides. The Wheat and the Weeds has a sower, an enemy, servants and reapers. The Growing Seed has a man who sleeps and rises. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The mustard seed moves from smallness to greatness. The leaven moves from hiding to the whole. The Sower moves through four kinds of ground, the Growing Seed from blade to harvest, and the Wheat and the Weeds from sowing to a delayed harvest. The Treasure and the Pearl move from finding to buying. None of those movements is read into these two.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the explanation Jesus gives for the seed in the Sower, and for the field in the Wheat and the Weeds, is not an explanation of the seed or the field here. Second, the leaven of the Pharisees in Luke 12:1 is not the leaven of Luke 13:21. Third, the birds that devour seed in the Sower’s account are not the birds that lodge in the branches, and no meaning is passed from one to the other.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17) and sent the Twelve to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:6). The mustard seed and the leaven belong to that ministry. The Kingdom they speak of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel. Luke’s account adds that the one who spoke was “journeying toward Jerusalem” (13:22).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parables were told before any of that, and they are not read as though the Body of Christ were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The “mysteries of the kingdom of heaven” that Jesus says are given to the disciples (Matthew 13:11) are His own Kingdom matters given to them during His earthly ministry. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul. The word is shared and the subject is not.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The tree, the branches, the birds, the leaven, the woman and the meal are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and the growth of the seed and the working of the leaven are not the Body’s salvation or its program. The parables are not read as a picture of how any person comes to God, and sowing, hiding and growing are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in these parables: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparisons show. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of patience about beginnings.** A small start is not a reason to despise what is begun. The seed begins as the least of seeds.',
    '**A lesson of attention to the end.** The parables ask a reader to look at where a thing arrives and not only where it starts.',
    '**A lesson about hidden work.** Work that is out of sight can be complete in its effect. A reader learns not to measure a thing only by what can be seen.',
    '**A lesson of humility.** Neither story says that the man or the woman makes the growth or the working happen. A reader learns that what is done in small and hidden ways is not to be turned into a claim of credit.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom’s growth is not turned into the Body’s program, and the leavening of the whole is not turned into a task laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its inheritance and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in these parables.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus compares the Kingdom to a grain of mustard seed that a man took and sowed or cast. It is the least of seeds, and when it has grown it is the greatest among herbs, a tree, and the birds or fowls of the air lodge in its branches. Matthew, Mark and Luke each tell it in their own words and setting. Jesus also compares the Kingdom to leaven that a woman took and hid in three measures of meal until the whole was leavened, and Matthew and Luke record that comparison. None of the three accounts records an explanation, and Jesus names none of the parts. The comparisons say that the Kingdom begins small, that the result is large, and that a hidden working reaches the whole. Jesus spoke them during His earthly Kingdom ministry to those who had heard that the Kingdom was at hand, before the Acts overlap and before the Mystery of the Body of Christ began with Paul. Anything added about the tree, the birds, the leaven, the woman or the meal goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a man takes a grain of mustard seed, which grows large and holds birds in its branches; a woman takes leaven and hides it in three measures of meal until the whole is leavened.',
    '**The meaning:** the Kingdom begins small, the result is large, and a hidden working reaches the whole.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus in any of the three accounts; no identity for the tree, the birds, the leaven, the woman or the meal; each gospel is read in its own setting.',
    '**The present lesson:** patience about beginnings, attention to the end, hidden work and humility, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

