# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 13
A(('h1', S13))
A(('p', '13.1 Related passages. Read each in its own setting and keep it apart from these parables.'))
A(('bul', [
    'Matthew 13:3–9 and 18–23, and Mark 4:3–20: the Sower, with Jesus’ explanation.',
    'Mark 4:26–29: the Growing Seed.',
    'Matthew 13:24–30 and 36–43: the Wheat and the Weeds, with Jesus’ explanation.',
    'Matthew 13:44–46: the Hidden Treasure and the Pearl.',
    'Luke 12:1: the leaven of the Pharisees, a different saying in a different setting.',
    'Matthew 4:17, Mark 1:15 and Matthew 10:5–7: what was preached before these parables.',
]))
A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Sower and the Soils, the Growing Seed, the Wheat and the Weeds, and the Hidden Treasure and the Pearl are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. These two parables make no use of their meanings, and they make none of these.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus compares the Kingdom to a grain of mustard seed that a man took and sowed, which is the least of seeds and becomes the greatest among herbs, a tree, with birds in its branches, and to leaven that a woman hid in three measures of meal until the whole was leavened. Matthew, Mark and Luke tell the mustard seed in their own words and settings, and Matthew and Luke give the leaven. Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that the Kingdom begins small, that the result is large, and that a hidden working reaches the whole.'))
A(('p', 'Write the synthesis in your own words in five to seven sentences. In it:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Tell the mustard seed and the leaven in their order.',
    'Say plainly which parts Jesus does not identify.',
    'Name one difference among the accounts.',
    'State what the comparison says about the Kingdom.',
    'State when Jesus told it and what He was preaching.',
    'End with one sentence naming what the parables do not say.',
]))
A(('call', 'Boundaries to Remember',
   'No explanation from Jesus, so no identity for the tree, the branches, the birds, the leaven, the woman or the meal. Each gospel is read in its own setting, and no detail is carried from one into another. Sowing, hiding and growing are actions in stories, not steps of salvation. The Body of Christ is none of the parts of these stories. The lessons are principles of conduct, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
